00:02:52.200 --> 00:02:53.706
(narrator) Verse 2, case 1, urllib3.

42
00:02:53.706 --> 00:03:01.357
(narrator) Each case spread has a left page, what happened, a right page, why, and a gold principle brick that snaps on across the bottom.

43
00:03:01.357 --> 00:03:09.309
(narrator) Throughout, blue bricks are reused packages, gold is your own code and what to keep, red is what breaks, and green is what is fixed.

44
00:03:09.309 --> 00:03:17.382
(narrator) What happened: git status says nothing to commit, yet a red error from docker says request got an unexpected keyword argument chunked.

45
00:03:17.382 --> 00:03:18.768
(narrator) Why: a tower of bricks.

46
00:03:18.768 --> 00:03:25.455
(narrator) The docker brick sits on the requests brick, which sits on urllib3 version 1.26: each depends on the one below.

47
00:03:25.455 --> 00:03:32.142
(narrator) Nothing is pinned, so a clean install swaps in a red urllib3 2.0.0 brick with different studs: its API changed.

48
00:03:32.142 --> 00:03:38.227
(narrator) requests no longer fits on it and slides off, then docker slides off requests, each with a red cross.

49
00:03:38.227 --> 00:03:41.962
(narrator) The principle brick: keep versions of your dependencies fixed.

50
00:03:41.962 --> 00:03:48.408
(narrator) A Pipfile line pins urllib3 below 2.0.0, version 1.26 slides back, and the tower settles with green checks.

51
00:03:48.408 --> 00:03:52.745
(narrator) A note: a lockfile is your parts list, every build gets the same bricks.

52
00:03:53.350 --> 00:03:55.160
♪ My code had not changed a line, ♪

53
00:03:55.160 --> 00:03:57.160
♪ Yet docker broke today. ♪

54
00:03:57.160 --> 00:03:59.240
♪ Docker calls on Requests; ♪

55
00:03:59.240 --> 00:04:01.500
♪ Requests calls urllib3. ♪

56
00:04:01.500 --> 00:04:03.660
♪ No version had been pinned, ♪

57
00:04:03.660 --> 00:04:05.700
♪ So a clean install got two; ♪

58
00:04:05.700 --> 00:04:08.030
♪ Version two changed its API, ♪

59
00:04:08.030 --> 00:04:09.880
♪ And Requests' calls broke too. ♪

60
00:04:09.880 --> 00:04:13.130
♪ Pin dependency versions: ♪

61
00:04:13.130 --> 00:04:15.980
♪ Nothing moves till you choose. ♪

62
00:04:16.900 --> 00:04:18.617
(narrator) Verse 3, case 2, Heartbleed.

63
00:04:18.617 --> 00:04:23.828
(narrator) A padlock labelled OpenSSL cracks, and a timeline shows the bug unseen for two years.

64
00:04:23.828 --> 00:04:29.223
(narrator) Why: a request asks the server to send back the letter a, with a length of 64 kilobytes.

65
00:04:29.223 --> 00:04:36.029
(narrator) The server trusts the length and sends back a long strip of its memory, including a password and a private key.

66
00:04:36.029 --> 00:04:40.811
(narrator) Your app is a small build: your code brick, with a check, on an OpenSSL brick.

67
00:04:40.811 --> 00:04:45.287
(narrator) An X-ray shows a crack in the OpenSSL brick: reused code brings its bugs.

68
00:04:45.287 --> 00:04:52.644
(narrator) The fix ships on 7 April 2014, and a grid of app bricks turns from red to green as each one updates: one fix, many apps.

69
00:04:52.644 --> 00:04:55.587
(narrator) The principle brick: pinned doesn't mean frozen.

70
00:04:55.587 --> 00:05:03.373
(narrator) The openssl brick moves from 1.0.1f to 1.0.1g, the tests pass, and the principle brick says update to receive security patches.

71
00:05:03.980 --> 00:05:05.560
♪ OpenSSL had a flaw; ♪

72
00:05:05.560 --> 00:05:07.820
♪ For two years, no one saw. ♪

73
00:05:07.820 --> 00:05:09.440
♪ A request claimed a length; ♪

74
00:05:09.440 --> 00:05:11.950
♪ The server trusted it ♪

75
00:05:11.950 --> 00:05:14.240
♪ And sent back its own memory: ♪

76
00:05:14.240 --> 00:05:15.760
♪ Passwords, private keys. ♪

77
00:05:15.760 --> 00:05:18.170
♪ I didn't write that bug, ♪

78
00:05:18.170 --> 00:05:20.570
♪ But I shipped it, all the same. ♪

79
00:05:20.570 --> 00:05:22.020
♪ The fix shipped within a week ♪

80
00:05:22.020 --> 00:05:24.230
♪ To all who chose to update; ♪

81
00:05:24.230 --> 00:05:26.340
♪ That's reuse on your side: ♪

82
00:05:26.340 --> 00:05:28.520
♪ One fix, shared by all. ♪

83
00:05:28.520 --> 00:05:31.180
♪ Pinned doesn't mean frozen: ♪

84
00:05:31.180 --> 00:05:32.433
♪ Update to the fix, then test. ♪

85
00:05:32.733 --> 00:05:37.292
(narrator) The refrain again: the checklist lights line by line as its tower of bricks goes up.

86
00:05:37.292 --> 00:05:40.657
(narrator) Case tabs now sit beside the pin line: urllib3 and Heartbleed.

87
