123
00:07:59.500 --> 00:08:01.322
(narrator) The bridge, case 4, Ariane 5.

124
00:08:01.322 --> 00:08:13.825
(narrator) Ariane 4, a white rocket on its launch table, carries a gold module, its inertial reference system, seen through a cut-away window, which stores horizontal velocity in a 16-bit integer, up to 32,767.

125
00:08:13.825 --> 00:08:16.464
(narrator) Ariane 4 flies, and the gauge stays green.

126
00:08:16.464 --> 00:08:23.187
(narrator) The same gold module is reused in Ariane 5, a bigger, faster rocket: the needle passes the limit, overflow.

127
00:08:23.187 --> 00:08:30.412
(narrator) At 37 seconds the rocket veers and breaks apart, its pieces scattering in a slow orange bloom: 370 million dollars.

128
00:08:30.412 --> 00:08:37.009
(narrator) A split card: Ariane 4 fits, Ariane 5 overflows, and a note: assumes horizontal velocity fits in 16 bits.

129
00:08:37.009 --> 00:08:51.335
(narrator) The principle brick: identify violated assumptions, with three boxes ticked in turn: check the documentation and code, check it was designed for your conditions, and test it, where a test bench catches the failure before launch.

130
00:08:51.335 --> 00:09:02.581
(narrator) Last, two arrows branch apart, and the module brick becomes two, version 1, 16-bit, and version 2, 32-bit, each with its own team: evolve separately, version it, or clone and own.

131
00:09:03.190 --> 00:09:05.380
♪ Code built for Ariane Four ♪

132
00:09:05.380 --> 00:09:07.100
♪ Stored horizontal speed ♪

133
00:09:07.100 --> 00:09:08.680
♪ In sixteen bits: enough, ♪

134
00:09:08.680 --> 00:09:11.080
♪ Since that rocket flew slower. ♪

135
00:09:11.080 --> 00:09:13.080
♪ Reused in Ariane Five, ♪

136
00:09:13.080 --> 00:09:15.260
