147
00:09:35.960 --> 00:09:38.120
♪ Version it, or clone and own: ♪

148
00:09:38.120 --> 00:09:40.867
♪ Copy it, so each can change. ♪

149
00:09:41.167 --> 00:09:43.269
(narrator) Verse 5, case 5, library or framework.

150
00:09:43.269 --> 00:09:50.957
(narrator) The left page shows two loose bricks, your code and axios, with the lecture's axios call and an arrow from your code to axios: you call it.

151
00:09:50.957 --> 00:10:00.637
(narrator) The right page shows a grey baseplate, Express, the framework, with a socket that your handler brick drops into, and an arrow from the baseplate to your handler: it calls you.

152
00:10:00.637 --> 00:10:04.121
(narrator) The principle brick: Hollywood Principle, inversion of control.

153
00:10:04.121 --> 00:10:06.666
(narrator) A GET request arrives and your handler lights.

154
00:10:06.666 --> 00:10:12.197
(narrator) The framework's own bricks, routing and server, go up around yours: it decides more, you write less.

155
00:10:12.197 --> 00:10:15.626
(narrator) A red dashed frame closes around the build: harder to reverse.

156
00:10:16.227 --> 00:10:17.807
♪ Library or framework? ♪

157
00:10:17.807 --> 00:10:19.367
♪ Ask who calls whom. ♪

158
00:10:19.367 --> 00:10:22.067
♪ I call axios when I choose; ♪

159
00:10:22.067 --> 00:10:23.727
♪ Express calls my code. ♪

160
00:10:23.727 --> 00:10:25.667
♪ That's the Hollywood Principle: ♪

161
00:10:25.667 --> 00:10:27.827
♪ Don't call us, we'll call you. ♪

162
00:10:27.827 --> 00:10:30.317
♪ It decides more; I write less, ♪

163
00:10:30.317 --> 00:10:32.167
♪ But it's harder to undo. ♪

164
00:10:32.467 --> 00:10:34.695
(narrator) The quiz: a fresh spread called your cases.

165
00:10:35.280 --> 00:10:37.667
♪ Your turn! Try these cases. ♪

166
00:10:37.967 --> 00:10:39.865
