169
00:10:51.728 --> 00:10:55.821
(narrator) 2: writing your own encryption, beside a vetted crypto library brick.

170
00:10:55.821 --> 00:10:59.558
(narrator) 3: a pinned library brick with a security fix in a new version.

171
00:10:59.558 --> 00:11:04.599
(narrator) 4: a date, 03/04/2026, read as March 4 by old code and meant as 3 April by new users.

172
00:11:04.599 --> 00:11:12.429
(narrator) 5: two app bricks standing on one shared module brick; when their needs drift apart, one app slides off, until the module is copied.

173
00:11:12.429 --> 00:11:15.039
(narrator) 6: pytest calling a test function you wrote.

174
00:11:15.650 --> 00:11:18.700
♪ A package just to check if a number is even? ♪

175
00:11:18.700 --> 00:11:21.280
♪ (crowd) No. It's one line: write it. ♪

176
00:11:21.280 --> 00:11:25.020
♪ Keep dependencies few, so write your own encryption? ♪

177
00:11:25.020 --> 00:11:27.220
♪ (crowd) No: reuse the tested one. ♪

178
00:11:27.220 --> 00:11:30.030
♪ Your pinned library gets a security fix? ♪

179
00:11:30.030 --> 00:11:31.840
♪ (crowd) Update on purpose, then test. ♪

180
00:11:31.840 --> 00:11:36.260
♪ Your old date code now runs for users in Europe? ♪

181
00:11:36.260 --> 00:11:38.370
♪ (crowd) Check if it assumes month first. ♪

182
00:11:38.370 --> 00:11:42.560
♪ Two apps share a module, and their needs drift apart? ♪

183
00:11:42.560 --> 00:11:44.320
♪ (crowd) Version it, or clone and own. ♪

184
00:11:44.320 --> 00:11:49.340
♪ pytest runs the tests you wrote: library or framework? ♪

185
00:11:49.340 --> 00:11:52.733
♪ (crowd) Framework: it calls your code. ♪

186
00:11:53.033 --> 00:12:03.991
(narrator) The final refrain: the checklist and its tower of bricks, now with a case tab beside every question it answers: Ariane 5 by assume, left-pad by pull in, urllib3 and Heartbleed by pin and update.

